 story=("The journey to the river","Every year Gauri's family travelled to a river bank to bathe and pray. Thousands of other families gathered there too.\nThey carried food and sang songs along the way.\n\"This is a journey to a sacred place,\" said her grandmother. \"We call it a pilgrimage.\""),
 explain=["People in India regard many rivers, mountains, forests, trees and places as sacred. Rivers such as the Ganga, Kaveri, Godavari and Yamuna, mountains such as the Himalayas and Kailash, and many temples, mosques, churches, gurdwaras and other shrines are visited by devotees.","A journey to a holy place is called a pilgrimage. Pilgrims travel on foot, by bullock cart or by train. Many fairs and festivals are held at sacred places, such as the Kumbh Mela, which draws crowds in millions.","Sacred geography links people with nature and with each other. It brings communities together, supports local trade and the economy, and asks us to protect these places and keep them clean."],
